[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65764,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Solitary fibrous tumour (SFT) is a fibroblastic tumour characterized by prominent thin-walled staghorn vasculature and <em>NAB2</em>-<em>STAT6</em> gene rearrangement.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65765,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8815/1 Solitary fibrous tumour NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65766,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F7C &amp; XH7E62 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of connective or other soft tissue &amp; Solitary fibrous tumour NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65767,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> haemangiopericytoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65768,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Solitary fibrous tumour, malignant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65769,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SFT uncommonly involves the female genital tract (FGT)&nbsp;&ndash; typically the vulva, vagina, or (rarely) cervix.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65770,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with a slow-growing mass {29106748}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65771,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SFTs of the lower FGT occur over a wide patient age range (22&ndash;75&nbsp;years), with peak incidence in the fifth decade of life {29106748}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65772,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65773,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The genetic hallmark of SFT is a paracentric inversion involving chromosome&nbsp;12q, resulting in fusion of <em>NAB2</em> and <em>STAT6</em> {23313954; 23761323; 23313952}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65774,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours vary in size from 1&nbsp;cm to &gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm {29106748} and are generally well circumscribed, with a firm, homogeneous, yellowish-tan cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65775,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SFT is composed of haphazardly arranged spindled to ovoid cells with indistinct, pale eosinophilic cytoplasm within a variably collagenous stroma, admixed with branching, staghorn-shaped (haemangiopericytoma-like) blood vessels. There is a wide histological spectrum, ranging from paucicellular lesions with abundant stromal keloidal-type collagen to highly cellular tumours consisting of closely spaced cells with little or no intervening stroma. Generally, cellularity is variable within the tumour. Histological patterns, including fat-forming and myxoid, have been described in the FGT. SFT is positive for CD34 and nuclear STAT6 {23575898; 24030747; 25873501}. STAT6 immunohistochemistry is a sensitive and specific surrogate for all fusions {24030747; 24702701; 23575898}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65776,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65777,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>NAB2</em>-<em>STAT6</em> gene fusions are pathognomonic for SFT {23313952; 23313954}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65778,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> spindled to ovoid-shaped cells arranged around a branching and hyalinized vasculature; variable stromal collagen deposition.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> STAT6 and CD34 expression; <em>NAB2</em>-<em>STAT6</em> fusion (in selected challenging cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65779,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Risk stratification models are preferred over anatomical staging.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:24:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65780,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 423,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>Recurrence occurs in 30% of cases {22575866; 29039030; 28838212; 26924782; 30206022}, with a rate as high as 40% seen after 5&nbsp;years {1203874; 9850176}; recurrence has rarely occurred after 15&nbsp;years {29039030}. Risk stratification models used in soft tissue tumours can be applied to SFT arising in the lower FGT. The most widely used multivariate model for metastatic risk incorporates mitotic count (&gt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>, equating to &gt;&nbsp;4&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.5&nbsp;mm in diameter and 0.2&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> in area), patient age (&gt;&nbsp;55&nbsp;years), and tumour size stratified by 5&nbsp;cm tiers to classify tumours into low-risk, intermediate-risk, and high-risk groups {22575866}. Adding necrosis as a variable results in higher numbers of cases being classified as low-risk {28731041; 29935303}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-05T09:32:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]